# 078 — Voice V1: offline command recognizer — done

**Type:** feature · **Owner:** tools track · **Priority:** P1 · **Status:** done (2026-10-04) · **Blocked by:** — · **Roadmap:** track G · **Constitution:** Art. 8.1–8.2, 11.11, MC-5

## Problem

Nothing turns speech into text. Voice control needs a recognizer of a fixed command grammar, in Russian and English, small enough for a 16 MiB program heap and fast enough on SSE2 ([docs/voice](../docs/voice/README.md) §4, V1).

## Plan

- Features: 40 log-mel bands (25 ms / 10 ms, 512-point FFT), per-utterance mean normalization; MFCC with deltas for the model.
- Phonemes: the synthesizer's letter-to-sound rules and lexicons move from `tts/` into a module shared by `tts` and the recognizer (no behaviour change for `tts`; its host tests guard that).
- Model: a small network (two convolutions, three dense layers, int8 weights, ≤ 3 MB) giving per-frame posteriors over the phoneme set plus silence; trained on the host by `scripts/voice_train.py` from speech synthesized by `tts` at several pitches and rates with noise; weights in `voice/model.bin` (header: version, phoneme set, feature parameters, checksum), loaded from the boot disk. Real recordings are a later addition (their licences go to `THIRD_PARTY.md`).
- Grammar: `voice/commands.txt` (phrases with slots, both languages) compiled into a phoneme graph; Viterbi decoding with a filler model for out-of-grammar speech; confidence from the best/second path; below the threshold the result is rejected.
- `hear [--wav file]` (console program, `REQUEST_CONSOLE`): records one utterance (077) and prints `HEARD "открой файлы" INTENT=open TOOL=fm CONFIDENCE=0.91` or `NOT UNDERSTOOD`.
- The recognizer gets only the audio client and reads its model and grammar through the application's read-only file client (Art. 11.11).

## Acceptance criteria

- Host test `tests/voice_host.rs`: every grammar phrase synthesized at three pitches and two rates with 20 dB SNR noise is recognized in ≥ 90 % of cases; 50 out-of-grammar phrases are rejected in ≥ 90 %; recognition of an 8 s utterance takes < 1 s on one host core with the kernel's build flags.
- QEMU: `hear --wav` on synthesized WAVs prints the right phrases.
- The model, its training script and its data provenance are in the repository; `THIRD_PARTY.md` covers anything not written here.

## Resolution

- **Phonetics shared:** `tts/src/text.rs`, `tts/src/phonemes.rs` and `tts/data/` moved into the crate `phonetics/` (used by `tts` and `hear`); the synthesizer's output is bit for bit what it was (checked on Russian and English phrases), and its host tests pass. `phonetics::phonemes::PHONES` names the recognizer's 37 phones (soft and hard consonants share a class); `synth::speak_labeled` tells which unit every chunk of samples belongs to.
- **Features** (`libmind/src/voice/features.rs`): 40 log-mel bands, 25 ms / 10 ms, 512-point FFT, pre-emphasis and Hamming window, per-utterance mean normalization — in integers, so the trainer and the target compute the same values.
- **Model** (`libmind/src/voice/model.rs`, `voice/model.bin` 260 051 bytes): a time convolution over 11 frames and three dense layers (440 → 256 → 256 → 256 → 38), int8 weights, integer requantization; the file carries version, feature parameters, the decoder's thresholds, the class names and an FNV-1a checksum.
- **Training** (`scripts/voice_train.rs`, `scripts/voice_corpus.rs`): every grammar phrase in six voices plus 4000 random strings of dictionary words, pitch 82–168 Hz, rate 75–135 %, silence or 8–35 dB white noise, phone labels from the synthesizer; f32 Adam for 12 epochs (about 7 minutes on 4 cores; held-out frame accuracy 96.3 % in f32 and in int8); thresholds calibrated on other voices and 24 other phrases outside the grammar (deficit ≤ 287/256 nats per frame; the recognized phrases' worst was 43, the outside phrases' best 543). Seeded: the same sources give the same file. `--calibrate` recomputes only the thresholds.
- **Grammar and decoding** (`voice/commands.txt`, `libmind/src/voice/grammar.rs`, `recognizer.rs`): 13 intents, a `tool` slot of 20 words, 147 phrases once filled in; chains of phone states (two frames at least, optional silence between words) decoded by Viterbi; the scores are relative to each frame's best class, so the free phone loop (the filler) scores 0 and a phrase's mean deficit per frame is its distance from it; the confidence is e^−deficit; the best phrase meaning something else gives the margin.
- **`hear`** (`hear/`, console program, `REQUEST_CONSOLE`; only the audio and the default read-only file clients): `hear [seconds]` (microphone, one utterance, default 8 s), `hear --wav FILE` (every utterance of the file); prints `HEARD "…" INTENT=… SLOT=… CONFIDENCE=…`, `NOT UNDERSTOOD (CLOSEST "…", CONFIDENCE=…)` or `NOTHING HEARD`. `02_build.sh` and the USB image put `voice/model.bin` and `voice/commands.txt` on the boot disk.
- **Tests:** `tests/voice_host.rs` (built with `-O` in CI): features of a tone, the model file's checks, the grammar's slots and errors; every grammar phrase at pitches 95/122/150 Hz and rates 85/115 % in 20 dB noise — 882 of 882 recognized (≥ 90 % required); 50 phrases outside the grammar, half of them close to commands («открой дверь», «перезагрузи страницу», "close the window") — none accepted (≥ 90 % rejected required); 8 s of speech recognized in about 70 ms on one host core. QEMU `listen` suite: `hear --wav` on a 48 kHz stereo WAV of «открой файлы», "what time is it" and «сегодня хорошая погода» made by the host build of `tts` prints two `HEARD` lines with the right intents and slot and one `NOT UNDERSTOOD`; `hear 1` hears nothing in the microphone's silence.
- **Deviations:** the trainer is Rust, not Python (no numpy here; it shares the target's feature and model code); stacked log-mel frames instead of MFCC with deltas; one time convolution instead of two; the 1-second budget is measured on the host's build at `-O` (the host target, not `x86_64-unknown-none`); slots are word lists, not free text. Provenance: the model is trained on our synthesizer's speech only and is listed in `THIRD_PARTY.md` (shared under CC BY-SA 4.0 to be safe, since the stress dictionary shaped that speech).
- **Limits:** one synthetic voice is all it has heard; real recordings come before V2 is offered to users (docs/voice §7).

